Build with C++20 on Linux, as onnxruntime already does on Darwin.

onnxruntime hardcodes CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD 17 for every platform except Darwin,
overriding any value a consumer passes in. That is incompatible with the abseil
vcpkg installs, which sets ABSL_OPTION_USE_STD_ORDERING=1 and therefore aliases
absl::strong_ordering to std::strong_ordering -- a C++20 type. Compiling
onnxruntime at C++17 against those headers fails with "'strong_ordering' has not
been declared in 'std'".

Upstream's own comment on this block reads "TODO: update this once all system
adapt c++20", and their Darwin branch is already on 20, so this extends the same
choice to Linux rather than inventing anything.

Only bites when onnxruntime is built from source. CI pulls it from the vcpkg
binary cache and never compiles it, which is why this went unnoticed.

diff --git a/cmake/CMakeLists.txt b/cmake/CMakeLists.txt
index 0b37ade..7f33666 100644
--- a/cmake/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/cmake/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -33,7 +33,7 @@ include(CheckSymbolExists)
 include(GNUInstallDirs) # onnxruntime_providers_* require CMAKE_INSTALL_* variables
 
 # TODO: update this once all system adapt c++20
-if(CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME STREQUAL "Darwin")
+if(CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME STREQUAL "Darwin" OR CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME STREQUAL "Linux")
 set(CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD 20)
 else()
 set(CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD 17)
